["class","<classname>EventBuffer<\/classname> represents Libevent's \"evbuffer\", an utility functionality for buffered I\/O.\n\nEvent buffers are meant to be generally useful for doing the \"buffer\" part of buffered network I\/O."]
["constant","The end of line is any sequence of any number of carriage return and linefeed characters. This format is not very useful; it exists mainly for backward compatibility."]
["constant","The end of the line is an optional carriage return, followed by a linefeed. (In other words, it is either a <literal>\"\\r\\n\"<\/literal> or a <literal>\"\\n\"<\/literal> .) This format is useful in parsing text-based Internet protocols, since the standards generally prescribe a <literal>\"\\r\\n\"<\/literal> line-terminator, but nonconformant clients sometimes say just <literal>\"\\n\"<\/literal>."]
["constant","The end of a line is a single carriage return, followed by a single linefeed. (This is also known as <literal>\"\\r\\n\"<\/literal>. The ASCII values are <literal>0x0D<\/literal> <literal>0x0A<\/literal> )."]
["constant","The end of a line is a single linefeed character. (This is also known as <literal>\"\\n\"<\/literal>. It is ASCII value is <literal>0x0A<\/literal> .)"]
["constant","The same as <constant>EventBuffer::PTR_SET<\/constant>, except this flag causes EventBuffer::setPosition method to move position forward up to the specified number of bytes(instead of setting absolute position)."]
["constant","Flag used as argument of EventBuffer::setPosition method. If this flag specified, the position pointer is moved to an absolute position within the buffer."]
["function","Constructs EventBuffer object\n\nConstructs EventBuffer object"]
["function","Append data to the end of an event buffer\n\nAppend data to the end of an event buffer.",[["$data","string","String to be appended to the end of the buffer."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::addBuffer"]]
["function","Move all data from a buffer provided to the current instance of EventBuffer\n\nMove all data from the buffer provided in <parameter>buf<\/parameter> parameter to the end of current <classname>EventBuffer<\/classname>. This is a destructive add. The data from one buffer moves into the other buffer. However, no unnecessary memory copies occur.",[["$buf","EventBuffer","The source EventBuffer object."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::add"]]
["function","Moves the specified number of bytes from a source buffer to the\n  end of the current buffer\n\nMoves the specified number of bytes from a source buffer to the end of the current buffer. If there are fewer number of bytes, it moves all the bytes available from the source buffer.",[["$buf","EventBuffer","Source buffer."],["$len","int",""]],["int","Returns the number of bytes read."],["EventBuffer::copyout","EventBuffer::drain","EventBuffer::pullup","EventBuffer::readLine","EventBuffer::read"]]
["function","Copies out specified number of bytes from the front of the buffer\n\nBehaves just like EventBuffer::read, but does not drain any data from the buffer. I.e. it copies the first <parameter>max_bytes<\/parameter> bytes from the front of the buffer into <parameter>data<\/parameter>. If there are fewer than <parameter>max_bytes<\/parameter> bytes available, the function copies all the bytes there are.",[["$data","string","Output string."],["$max_bytes","int","The number of bytes to copy."]],["int","Returns the number of bytes copied, or <constant>-1<\/constant> on failure."],["EventBuffer::read","EventBuffer::appendFrom"]]
["function","Removes specified number of bytes from the front of the buffer\n  without copying it anywhere\n\nBehaves as EventBuffer::read, except that it does not copy the data: it just removes it from the front of the buffer.",[["$len","int","The number of bytes to remove from the buffer."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::read","EventBuffer::appendFrom"]]
["function","Enable locking on an <classname>EventBuffer<\/classname> so that it can safely be used by multiple threads at the same time. When locking is enabled, the lock will be held when callbacks are invoked. This could result in deadlock if you aren't careful. Plan accordingly!",null,["void","No value is returned."],["Evbuffers and Thread-safety"]]
["function","Reserves space in buffer\n\nAlters the last chunk of memory in the buffer, or adds a new chunk, such that the buffer is now large enough to contain <parameter>len<\/parameter> bytes without any further allocations.",[["$len","int","The number of bytes to reserve for the buffer"]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."]]
["function","Prevent calls that modify an event buffer from succeeding\n\nPrevent calls that modify an event buffer from succeeding",[["$at_front","bool","Whether to disable changes to the front or end of the buffer."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::unfreeze"]]
["function","Acquires a lock on buffer\n\nAcquires a lock on buffer. Can be used in pair with EventBuffer::unlock to make a set of operations atomic, i.e. thread-safe. Note, it is not needed to lock buffers for individual operations. When locking is enabled(see EventBuffer::enableLocking ), individual operations on event buffers are already atomic.",null,["void","No value is returned."],["EventBuffer::unlock"]]
["function","Prepend data to the front of the buffer\n\nPrepend data to the front of the buffer.",[["$data","string","String to be prepended to the front of the buffer."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::prependBuffer","EventBuffer::add","EventBuffer::addBuffer"]]
["function","Moves all data from source buffer to the front of current buffer\n\nBehaves as EventBuffer::addBuffer, except that it moves data to the front of the buffer.",[["$buf","EventBuffer","Source buffer."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::add","EventBuffer::addBuffer","EventBuffer::prepend"]]
["function","Linearizes data within buffer\n  and returns its contents as a string\n\n\"Linearizes\" the first <parameter>size<\/parameter> bytes of the buffer, copying or moving them as needed to ensure that they are all contiguous and occupying the same chunk of memory. If size is negative, the function linearizes the entire buffer.\n\nCalling EventBuffer::pullup with a large size can be quite slow, since it potentially needs to copy the entire buffer's contents.",[["$size","int","The number of bytes required to be contiguous within the buffer."]],["string","If <parameter>size<\/parameter> is greater than the number of bytes in the buffer, the function returns <type>null<\/type>. Otherwise, EventBuffer::pullup returns string."],["EventBuffer::copyout","EventBuffer::drain","EventBuffer::read","EventBuffer::readLine","EventBuffer::appendFrom"]]
["function","Read data from an evbuffer and drain the bytes read\n\nRead the first <parameter>max_bytes<\/parameter> from the buffer and drain the bytes read. If more <parameter>max_bytes<\/parameter> are requested than are available in the buffer, it only extracts as many bytes as available.",[["$max_bytes","int","Maximum number of bytes to read from the buffer."]],["string","Returns string read, or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::copyout","EventBuffer::drain","EventBuffer::pullup","EventBuffer::readLine","EventBuffer::appendFrom"]]
["function","Read data from a file onto the end of the buffer\n\nRead data from the file specified by <parameter>fd<\/parameter> onto the end of the buffer.",[["$fd","mixed","Socket resource, stream, or numeric file descriptor."],["$howmuch","int","Maximum number of bytes to read."]],["int","Returns the number of bytes read, or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::copyout","EventBuffer::drain","EventBuffer::pullup","EventBuffer::readLine","EventBuffer::appendFrom"]]
["function","Extracts a line from the front of the buffer\n\nExtracts a line from the front of the buffer and returns it in a newly allocated string. If there is not a whole line to read, the function returns <type>null<\/type>. The line terminator is not included in the copied string.",[["$eol_style","int","One of EventBuffer:EOL_* constants."]],["string","On success returns the line read from the buffer, otherwise <type>null<\/type>."],["EventBuffer::copyout","EventBuffer::drain","EventBuffer::pullup","EventBuffer::read","EventBuffer::appendFrom"]]
["function","Scans the buffer for an occurrence of a string\n\nScans the buffer for an occurrence of the string <parameter>what<\/parameter>. It returns numeric position of the string, or <type>false<\/type> if the string was not found.\n\nIf the <parameter>start<\/parameter> argument is provided, it points to the position at which the search should begin; otherwise, the search is performed from the start of the string. If <parameter>end<\/parameter> argument provided, the search is performed between start and end buffer positions.",[["$what","string","String to search."],["$start","int","Start search position."],["$end","int","End search position."]],["mixed","Returns numeric position of the first occurrence of the string in the buffer, or <type>false<\/type> if string is not found.\n\nThis function may return Boolean <type>false<\/type>, but may also return a non-Boolean value which evaluates to <type>false<\/type>. Please read the section on Booleans for more information. Use the === operator for testing the return value of this function."],["EventBuffer::searchEol"]]
["function","Scans the buffer for an occurrence of an end of line\n\nScans the buffer for an occurrence of an end of line specified by <parameter>eol_style<\/parameter> parameter . It returns numeric position of the string, or <type>false<\/type> if the string was not found.\n\nIf the <parameter>start<\/parameter> argument is provided, it represents the position at which the search should begin; otherwise, the search is performed from the start of the string. If <parameter>end<\/parameter> argument provided, the search is performed between start and end buffer positions.",[["$start","int","Start search position."],["$eol_style","int","One of EventBuffer:EOL_* constants."]],["mixed","Returns numeric position of the first occurrence of end-of-line symbol in the buffer, or <type>false<\/type> if not found.\n\nThis function may return Boolean <type>false<\/type>, but may also return a non-Boolean value which evaluates to <type>false<\/type>. Please read the section on Booleans for more information. Use the === operator for testing the return value of this function."],["EventBuffer::search"]]
["function","Subtracts a portion of the buffer data\n\nSubtracts up to <parameter>length<\/parameter> bytes of the buffer data beginning at <parameter>start<\/parameter> position.",[["$start","int","The start position of data to be subtracted."],["$length","int","Maximum number of bytes to subtract."]],["string","Returns the data subtracted as a <type>string<\/type> on success, or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::read"]]
["function","Re-enable calls that modify an event buffer\n\nRe-enable calls that modify an event buffer.",[["$at_front","bool","Whether to enable events at the front or at the end of the buffer."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::freeze"]]
["function","Releases lock acquired by EventBuffer::lock\n\nReleases lock acquired by EventBuffer::lock.",null,["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBuffer::lock"]]
["function","Write contents of the buffer to a file or socket\n\nWrite contents of the buffer to a file descriptor. The buffer will be drained after the bytes have been successfully written.",[["$fd","mixed","Socket resource, stream or numeric file descriptor normally associated with a socket."],["$howmuch","int","The maximum number of bytes to write."]],["int","Returns the number of bytes written, or <type>false<\/type> on error."],["EventBuffer::read"]]
["class","Represents Libevent's buffer event.\n\nUsually an application wants to perform some amount of data buffering in addition to just responding to events. When we want to write data, for example, the usual pattern looks like:\n\nDecide that we want to write some data to a connection; put that data in a buffer.\n\nWait for the connection to become writable\n\nWrite as much of the data as we can\n\nRemember how much we wrote, and if we still have more data to write, wait for the connection to become writable again.\n\nThis buffered I\/O pattern is common enough that Libevent provides a generic mechanism for it. A \"buffer event\" consists of an underlying transport (like a socket), a read buffer, and a write buffer. Instead of regular events, which give callbacks when the underlying transport is ready to be read or written, a buffer event invokes its user-supplied callbacks when it has read or written enough data."]
["constant","Finished a requested connection on the bufferevent."]
["constant","Got an end-of-file indication on the buffer event."]
["constant","An error occurred during a bufferevent operation. For more information on what the error was, call EventUtil::getLastSocketErrno and\/or EventUtil::getLastSocketError."]
["constant","When the buffer event is freed, close the underlying transport. This will close an underlying socket, free an underlying buffer event, etc."]
["constant","When this flag is set, the bufferevent defers all of its callbacks. See Fast portable non-blocking network programming with Libevent, Deferred callbacks."]
["constant","Automatically allocate locks for the bufferevent, so that it’s safe to use from multiple threads."]
["constant","By default, when the bufferevent is set up to be threadsafe, the buffer event’s locks are held whenever the any user-provided callback is invoked. Setting this option makes Libevent release the buffer event’s lock when it’s invoking the callbacks."]
["constant","An event occurred during a read operation on the bufferevent. See the other flags for which event it was."]
["constant","SSL is currently performing negotiation as a server"]
["constant","SSL is currently performing negotiation as a client"]
["constant","The SSL handshake is done"]
["constant","An event occurred during a write operation on the bufferevent. See the other flags for which event it was."]
["function","Constructs EventBufferEvent object\n\nCreate a buffer event on a socket, stream or a file descriptor. Passing <type>null<\/type> to <parameter>socket<\/parameter> means that the socket should be created later, e.g. by means of EventBufferEvent::connect.",[["$base","EventBase","Event base that should be associated with the new buffer event."],["$socket","mixed","May be created as a stream(not necessarily by means of <literal>sockets<\/literal> extension)"],["$options","int","One of EventBufferEvent::OPT_* constants, or <literal>0<\/literal>."],["$readcb","callable","Read event callback. See About buffer event callbacks."],["$writecb","callable","Write event callback. See About buffer event callbacks."],["$eventcb","callable","Status-change event callback. See About buffer event callbacks."],["$arg","mixed","A variable that will be passed to all the callbacks."]],null,["EventBufferEvent::sslFilter","EventBufferEvent::sslSocket"]]
["function","Closes file descriptor associated with the current buffer event\n\nCloses file descriptor associated with the current buffer event.\n\nThis method may be used in cases when the <constant>EventBufferEvent::OPT_CLOSE_ON_FREE<\/constant> option is not appropriate.",null,["void","No value is returned."]]
["function","Connect buffer event's file descriptor to given address or\n  UNIX socket\n\nConnect buffer event's file descriptor to given address(optionally with port), or a UNIX domain socket.\n\nIf socket is not assigned to the buffer event, this function allocates a new socket and makes it non-blocking internally.\n\nTo resolve DNS names(asynchronously), use EventBufferEvent::connectHost method.",[["$addr","string",["Should contain an IP address with optional port number, or a path to UNIX domain socket. Recognized formats are:",{"type":"code","text":"[IPv6Address]:port\n[IPv6Address]\nIPv6Address\nIPv4Address:port\nIPv4Address\nunix:path","kind":"screen"},"Note, <literal>'unix:'<\/literal> prefix is currently not case sensitive."]]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBufferEvent::connectHost"]]
["function","Connects to a hostname with optionally asynchronous DNS resolving\n\nResolves the DNS name hostname, looking for addresses of type <parameter>family<\/parameter> ( <literal>EventUtil::AF_*<\/literal> constants). If the name resolution fails, it invokes the event callback with an error event. If it succeeds, it launches a connection attempt just as EventBufferEvent::connect would.\n\n<parameter>dns_base<\/parameter> is optional. May be <type>null<\/type>, or an object created with EventDnsBase::__construct. For asynchronous hostname resolving pass a valid event dns base resource. Otherwise the hostname resolving will block.\n\n<classname>EventDnsBase<\/classname> is available only if <literal>Event<\/literal> configured --with-event-extra ( <literal>event_extra<\/literal> library, libevent protocol-specific functionality support including HTTP, DNS, and RPC ).\n\nEventBufferEvent::connectHost requires <literal>libevent-2.0.3-alpha<\/literal> or greater.",[["$dns_base","EventDnsBase","Object of <classname>EventDnsBase<\/classname> in case if DNS is to be resolved asynchronously. Otherwise <type>null<\/type>."],["$hostname","string",["Hostname to connect to. Recognized formats are:",{"type":"code","text":"www.example.com (hostname)\n 1.2.3.4 (ipv4address)\n ::1 (ipv6address)\n[::1] ([ipv6address])","kind":"screen"}]],["$port","int","Port number"],["$family","int","Address family. <constant>EventUtil::AF_UNSPEC<\/constant>, <constant>EventUtil::AF_INET<\/constant>, or <constant>EventUtil::AF_INET6<\/constant>. See EventUtil constants."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBufferEvent::connect"]]
["function","Creates two buffer events connected to each other\n\nReturns array of two <classname>EventBufferEvent<\/classname> objects connected to each other. All the usual options are supported, except for <constant>EventBufferEvent::OPT_CLOSE_ON_FREE<\/constant>, which has no effect, and <constant>EventBufferEvent::OPT_DEFER_CALLBACKS<\/constant>, which is always on.",[["$base","EventBase","Associated event base"],["$options","int","EventBufferEvent::OPT_* constants combined with bitwise <literal>OR<\/literal> operator."]],["array","Returns array of two <classname>EventBufferEvent<\/classname> objects connected to each other."]]
["function","Disable events read, write, or both on a buffer event\n\nDisable events <constant>Event::READ<\/constant>, <constant>Event::WRITE<\/constant>, or <constant>Event::READ<\/constant> <literal>|<\/literal> <constant>Event::WRITE<\/constant> on a buffer event.",[["$events","int",""]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBufferEvent::enable"]]
["function","Enable events read, write, or both on a buffer event\n\nEnable events <constant>Event::READ<\/constant>, <constant>Event::WRITE<\/constant>, or <constant>Event::READ<\/constant> <literal>|<\/literal> <constant>Event::WRITE<\/constant> on a buffer event.",[["$events","int","<constant>Event::READ<\/constant>, <constant>Event::WRITE<\/constant>, or <constant>Event::READ<\/constant><literal>|<\/literal><constant>Event::WRITE<\/constant> on a buffer event."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["EventBufferEvent::disable"]]
["function","Free a buffer event\n\nFree resources allocated by buffer event.\n\nUsually there is no need to call this method, since normally it is done within internal object destructors. However, sometimes we have a long-time script allocating lots of instances, or a script with a heavy memory usage, where we need to free resources as soon as possible. In such cases EventBufferEvent::free may be used to protect the script against running up to the <literal>memory_limit<\/literal>.",null,["void","No value is returned."]]
["function","Returns string describing the last failed DNS lookup attempt\n\nReturns string describing the last failed DNS lookup attempt made by EventBufferEvent::connectHost, or an empty string, if there is no DNS error detected.",null,["string","Returns a string describing DNS lookup error, or an empty string for no error."],["EventBufferEvent::connectHost"]]
