["constant","The igbinary serializer. Instead of textual representation it stores PHP data structures in a compact binary form, resulting in space and time gains.\n\nThis serializer is supported only if memcached is configured with --enable-memcached-igbinary option and the igbinary extension is loaded."]
["constant","The JSON serializer. This serializer deserializes JSON to an object."]
["constant","The JSON array serializer. This serializer deserializes JSON to an associative array"]
["constant","The MessagePack serializer.\n\nThis serializer is supported only if memcached is configured with --enable-memcached-msgpack option and the <literal>msgpack<\/literal> extension is loaded."]
["constant","The default PHP serializer."]
["function","Create a Memcached instance\n\nCreates a Memcached instance representing the connection to the memcache servers.\n\nThis function is currently not documented; only its argument list is available.",[["$persistent_id","mixed","By default the Memcached instances are destroyed at the end of the request. To create an instance that persists between requests, use <parameter>persistent_id<\/parameter> to specify a unique ID for the instance. All instances created with the same <parameter>persistent_id<\/parameter> will share the same connection."],["$callback","mixed","callback(<type>Memcached<\/type> <parameter>memcached<\/parameter>, <type>string<\/type>|<type>null<\/type> <parameter>persistent_id<\/parameter>): <type>void<\/type>\n\nThe <parameter>callback<\/parameter> parameter is called when the connection is established. It should be a valid PHP <type>callable<\/type> that receive the <classname>Memcached<\/classname> object as its first parameter, and <parameter>persistent_id<\/parameter> as its second."],["$connection_str","mixed","This parameter is used to pass additional connection options to the memcache servers, such as a server weight in a cluster."]]]
["function","Add an item under a new key\n\n<function>Memcached::add<\/function> is similar to Memcached::set, but the operation fails if the <parameter>key<\/parameter> already exists on the server.",[["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTSTORED<\/constant> if the key already exists."],["Memcached::addByKey","Memcached::set","Memcached::replace"]]
["function","Add an item under a new key on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::addByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::add, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server. This is useful if you need to keep a bunch of related keys on a certain server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTSTORED<\/constant> if the key already exists."],["Memcached::add","Memcached::set","Memcached::replace"]]
["function","Add a server to the server pool\n\n<function>Memcached::addServer<\/function> adds the specified server to the server pool. No connection is established to the server at this time, but if you are using consistent key distribution option (via <constant>Memcached::DISTRIBUTION_CONSISTENT<\/constant> or <constant>Memcached::OPT_LIBKETAMA_COMPATIBLE<\/constant>), some of the internal data structures will have to be updated. Thus, if you need to add multiple servers, it is better to use Memcached::addServers as the update then happens only once.\n\nThe same server may appear multiple times in the server pool, because no duplication checks are made. This is not advisable; instead, use the <parameter>weight<\/parameter> option to increase the selection weighting of this server.",[["$host","string","The hostname of the memcache server. If the hostname is invalid, data-related operations will set <constant>Memcached::RES_HOST_LOOKUP_FAILURE<\/constant> result code. As of version 2.0.0b1, this parameter may also specify the path of a unix socket filepath ex. <literal>\/path\/to\/memcached.sock<\/literal> to use UNIX domain sockets, in this case <parameter>port<\/parameter> must also be set to <literal>0<\/literal>."],["$port","int","The port on which memcache is running. Usually, this is <literal>11211<\/literal>. As of version 2.0.0b1, set this parameter to <literal>0<\/literal> when using UNIX domain sockets."],["$weight","int","The weight of the server relative to the total weight of all the servers in the pool. This controls the probability of the server being selected for operations. This is used only with consistent distribution option and usually corresponds to the amount of memory available to memcache on that server."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::addServers","Memcached::resetServerList"]]
["function","Add multiple servers to the server pool\n\n<function>Memcached::addServers<\/function> adds <parameter>servers<\/parameter> to the server pool. Each entry in <parameter>servers<\/parameter> is supposed to be an array containing hostname, port, and, optionally, weight of the server. No connection is established to the servers at this time.\n\nThe same server may appear multiple times in the server pool, because no duplication checks are made. This is not advisable; instead, use the <parameter>weight<\/parameter> option to increase the selection weighting of this server.",[["$array","mixed","Array of the servers to add to the pool."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::addServer","Memcached::resetServerList"]]
["function","Append data to an existing item\n\n<function>Memcached::append<\/function> appends the given <parameter>value<\/parameter> string to the value of an existing item. The reason that <parameter>value<\/parameter> is forced to be a string is that appending mixed types is not well-defined.\n\nIf the <constant>Memcached::OPT_COMPRESSION<\/constant> is enabled, the operation will fail and a warning will be issued, because appending compressed data to a value that is potentially already compressed is not possible.",[["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","string","The string to append."]],["bool|null","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Returns <type>null<\/type> when compression is on."],["Memcached::appendByKey","Memcached::prepend"]]
["function","Append data to an existing item on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::appendByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::append, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","string","The string to append."]],["bool|null","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Returns <type>null<\/type> when compression is on."],["Memcached::append","Memcached::prepend"]]
["function","Compare and swap an item\n\n<function>Memcached::cas<\/function> performs a \"check and set\" operation, so that the item will be stored only if no other client has updated it since it was last fetched by this client. The check is done via the <parameter>cas_token<\/parameter> parameter which is a unique 64-bit value assigned to the existing item by memcache. See the documentation for Memcached::get* methods for how to obtain this token. Note that the token is represented as a float due to the limitations of PHP's integer space.",[["$cas_token","string|int|float","Unique value associated with the existing item. Generated by memcache."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_DATA_EXISTS<\/constant> if the item you are trying to store has been modified since you last fetched it."],["Memcached::casByKey"]]
["function","Compare and swap an item on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::casByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::cas, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server. This is useful if you need to keep a bunch of related keys on a certain server.",[["$cas_token","string|int|float","Unique value associated with the existing item. Generated by memcache."],["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_DATA_EXISTS<\/constant> if the item you are trying to store has been modified since you last fetched it."],["Memcached::cas"]]
["function","Decrement numeric item's value\n\n<function>Memcached::decrement<\/function> decrements a numeric item's value by the specified <parameter>offset<\/parameter>. If the item's value is not numeric, an error will result. If the operation would decrease the value below 0, the new value will be 0. <function>Memcached::decrement<\/function> will set the item to the <parameter>initial_value<\/parameter> parameter if the key doesn't exist.",[["$key","string","The key of the item to decrement."],["$offset","int","The amount by which to decrement the item's value."],["$initial_value","int","The value to set the item to if it doesn't currently exist."],["$expiry","int","The expiry time to set on the item."]],["int|false","Returns item's new value on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::increment","Memcached::incrementByKey","Memcached::decrementByKey"]]
["function","Decrement numeric item's value, stored on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::decrementByKey<\/function> decrements a numeric item's value by the specified <parameter>offset<\/parameter>. If the item's value is not numeric, an error will result. If the operation would decrease the value below 0, the new value will be 0. <function>Memcached::decrementByKey<\/function> will set the item to the <parameter>initial_value<\/parameter> parameter if the key doesn't exist.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key of the item to decrement."],["$offset","int","The amount by which to decrement the item's value."],["$initial_value","int","The value to set the item to if it doesn't currently exist."],["$expiry","int","The expiry time to set on the item."]],["int|false","Returns item's new value on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::decrement","Memcached::increment","Memcached::incrementByKey"]]
["function","Delete an item\n\nDelete the <parameter>key<\/parameter> from the server.",[["$key","string","The key to be deleted."],["$time","int","The amount of time the server will wait to delete the item.\n\nAs of memcached 1.3.0 (released 2009) this feature is no longer supported. Passing a non-zero <parameter>time<\/parameter> will cause the deletion to fail. Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>MEMCACHED_INVALID_ARGUMENTS<\/constant>."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTFOUND<\/constant> if the key does not exist."],["Memcached::deleteByKey","Memcached::deleteMulti"]]
["function","Delete an item from a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::deleteByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::delete, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key to be deleted."],["$time","int","The amount of time the server will wait to delete the item.\n\nAs of memcached 1.3.0 (released 2009) this feature is no longer supported. Passing a non-zero <parameter>time<\/parameter> will cause the deletion to fail. Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>MEMCACHED_INVALID_ARGUMENTS<\/constant>."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTFOUND<\/constant> if the key does not exist."],["Memcached::delete","Memcached::deleteMulti","Memcached::deleteMultiByKey"]]
["function","Delete multiple items\n\nDelete the array of <parameter>keys<\/parameter> from the server.",[["$keys","array","The keys to be deleted."],["$time","int","The amount of time the server will wait to delete the items.\n\nAs of memcached 1.3.0 (released 2009) this feature is no longer supported. Passing a non-zero <parameter>time<\/parameter> will cause the deletion to fail. Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>MEMCACHED_INVALID_ARGUMENTS<\/constant>."]],["array","Returns an array indexed by <parameter>keys<\/parameter>. Each element is <type>true<\/type> if the corresponding key was deleted, or one of the <constant>Memcached::RES_*<\/constant> constants if the corresponding deletion failed.\n\nThe Memcached::getResultCode will return the result code for the last executed delete operation, that is, the delete operation for the last element of <parameter>keys<\/parameter>."],["Memcached::delete","Memcached::deleteByKey","Memcached::deleteMultiByKey"]]
["function","Delete multiple items from a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::deleteMultiByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::deleteMulti, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>keys<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$keys","array","The keys to be deleted."],["$time","int","The amount of time the server will wait to delete the items.\n\nAs of memcached 1.3.0 (released 2009) this feature is no longer supported. Passing a non-zero <parameter>time<\/parameter> will cause the deletion to fail. Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>MEMCACHED_INVALID_ARGUMENTS<\/constant>."]],["array","Returns an array indexed by <parameter>keys<\/parameter>. Each element is <type>true<\/type> if the corresponding key was deleted, or one of the <constant>Memcached::RES_*<\/constant> constants if the corresponding deletion failed.\n\nThe Memcached::getResultCode will return the result code for the last executed delete operation, that is, the delete operation for the last element of <parameter>keys<\/parameter>."],["Memcached::delete","Memcached::deleteByKey","Memcached::deleteMulti"]]
["function","Fetch the next result\n\n<function>Memcached::fetch<\/function> retrieves the next result from the last request.",null,["array|false","Returns the next result or <type>false<\/type> otherwise. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_END<\/constant> if result set is exhausted."],["Memcached::fetchAll","Memcached::getDelayed"]]
["function","Fetch all the remaining results\n\n<function>Memcached::fetchAll<\/function> retrieves all the remaining results from the last request.",null,["array|false","Returns the results or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::fetch","Memcached::getDelayed"]]
["function","Invalidate all items in the cache\n\n<function>Memcached::flush<\/function> invalidates all existing cache items immediately (by default) or after the <parameter>delay<\/parameter> specified. After invalidation none of the items will be returned in response to a retrieval command (unless it's stored again under the same key after <function>Memcached::flush<\/function> has invalidated the items). The flush does not actually free all the memory taken up by the existing items; that will happen gradually as new items are stored.",[["$delay","int","Number of seconds to wait before invalidating the items."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."]]
["function","Retrieve an item\n\n<function>Memcached::get<\/function> returns the item that was previously stored under the <parameter>key<\/parameter>. If the item is found and the <parameter>get_flags<\/parameter> is given <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant>, it will also return the CAS token value for the item. See Memcached::cas for how to use CAS tokens. Read-through caching callback may be specified via <parameter>cache_cb<\/parameter> parameter.",[["$key","string","The key of the item to retrieve."],["$cache_cb","callable|null","Read-through caching callback or <type>null<\/type>."],["$get_flags","int","Flags to control the returned result. When <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant> is given, the function will also return the CAS token."]],["mixed","Returns the value stored in the cache or <type>false<\/type> otherwise. If the <parameter>get_flags<\/parameter> is set to <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant>, an array containing the value and the CAS token is returned instead of only the value. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTFOUND<\/constant> if the key does not exist."],["Memcached::getByKey","Memcached::getMulti","Memcached::getDelayed"]]
["function","Gets the keys stored on all the servers\n\n<function>Memcached::getAllKeys<\/function> queries each memcache server and retrieves an array of all keys stored on them at that point in time. This is not an atomic operation, so it isn't a truly consistent snapshot of the keys at point in time. As memcache doesn't guarantee to return all keys you also cannot assume that all keys have been returned.\n\nThis method is intended for debugging purposes and should not be used at scale!",null,["array|false","Returns the keys stored on all the servers on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."]]
["function","Retrieve an item from a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::getByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::get, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key of the item to fetch."],["$cache_cb","callable|null","Read-through caching callback or <type>null<\/type>"],["$get_flags","int","Flags to control the returned result. When value of <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant> is given will return the CAS token."]],["mixed","Returns the value stored in the cache or <type>false<\/type> otherwise. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTFOUND<\/constant> if the key does not exist."],["Memcached::get","Memcached::getMulti","Memcached::getDelayed"]]
["function","Request multiple items\n\n<function>Memcached::getDelayed<\/function> issues a request to memcache for multiple items the keys of which are specified in the <parameter>keys<\/parameter> array. The method does not wait for response and returns right away. When you are ready to collect the items, call either Memcached::fetch or Memcached::fetchAll. If <parameter>with_cas<\/parameter> is true, the CAS token values will also be requested.\n\nInstead of fetching the results explicitly, you can specify a result callback via <parameter>value_cb<\/parameter> parameter.",[["$keys","array","Array of keys to request."],["$with_cas","bool","Whether to request CAS token values also."],["$value_cb","callable|null","The result callback or <type>null<\/type>."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::getDelayedByKey","Memcached::fetch","Memcached::fetchAll"]]
["function","Request multiple items from a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::getDelayedByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::getDelayed, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>keys<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$keys","array","Array of keys to request."],["$with_cas","bool","Whether to request CAS token values also."],["$value_cb","callable|null","The result callback or <type>null<\/type>."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::getDelayed","Memcached::fetch","Memcached::fetchAll"]]
["function","Retrieve multiple items\n\n<function>Memcached::getMulti<\/function> is similar to Memcached::get, but instead of a single key item, it retrieves multiple items the keys of which are specified in the <parameter>keys<\/parameter> array.  Before v3.0 a second argument <parameter role=\"reference\">cas_tokens<\/parameter> was in use. It was filled with the CAS token values for the found items. The <parameter role=\"reference\">cas_tokens<\/parameter> parameter was removed in v3.0 of the extension. It was replaced with a new flag <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant> that needs is to be used as the value for <parameter>get_flags<\/parameter>. \n\nBefore v3.0 a second argument <parameter role=\"reference\">cas_tokens<\/parameter> was in use. It was filled with the CAS token values for the found items. The <parameter role=\"reference\">cas_tokens<\/parameter> parameter was removed in v3.0 of the extension. It was replaced with a new flag <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant> that needs is to be used as the value for <parameter>get_flags<\/parameter>.\n\nThe <parameter>get_flags<\/parameter> parameter can be used to specify additional options for <function>Memcached::getMulti<\/function>. <constant>Memcached::GET_PRESERVE_ORDER<\/constant> ensures that the keys are returned in the same order as they were requested in. <constant>Memcached::GET_EXTENDED<\/constant> ensures that the CAS tokens will be fetched too.",[["$keys","array","Array of keys to retrieve."],["$get_flags","int","The flags for the get operation."]],["array|false","Returns the array of found items or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::getMultiByKey","Memcached::get","Memcached::getDelayed"]]
["function","Retrieve multiple items from a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::getMultiByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::getMulti, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>keys<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$keys","array","Array of keys to retrieve."],["$get_flags","int","The flags for the get operation."]],["array|false","Returns the array of found items or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::getMulti","Memcached::get","Memcached::getDelayed"]]
["function","Retrieve a Memcached option value\n\nThis method returns the value of a Memcached <parameter>option<\/parameter>. Some options correspond to the ones defined by libmemcached, and some are specific to the extension. See Memcached Constants for more information.",[["$option","int","One of the <literal>Memcached::OPT_*<\/literal> constants."]],["mixed","Returns the value of the requested option, or <type>false<\/type> on error."],["Memcached::getOption","Memcached::setOption","Memcached Constants"]]
["function","Return the result code of the last operation\n\n<function>Memcached::getResultCode<\/function> returns one of the <constant>Memcached::RES_*<\/constant> constants that is the result of the last executed Memcached method.",null,["int","Result code of the last Memcached operation."]]
["function","Return the message describing the result of the last operation\n\n<function>Memcached::getResultMessage<\/function> returns a string that describes the result code of the last executed Memcached method.",null,["string","Message describing the result of the last Memcached operation."]]
["function","Map a key to a server\n\n<function>Memcached::getServerByKey<\/function> returns the server that would be selected by a particular <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> in all the <function>Memcached::*ByKey<\/function> operations.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."]],["array|false","Returns an array containing three keys of <literal>host<\/literal>, <literal>port<\/literal>, and <literal>weight<\/literal> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."]]
["function","Get the list of the servers in the pool\n\n<function>Memcached::getServerList<\/function> returns the list of all servers that are in its server pool.",null,["array","The list of all servers in the server pool."]]
["function","Get server pool statistics\n\n<function>Memcached::getStats<\/function> returns an array containing the state of all available memcache servers. See memcache protocol specification for details on these statistics.",[["$type","string|null","The type of statistics to fetch."]],["array|false","Array of server statistics, one entry per server, or <type>false<\/type> on failure."]]
["function","Get server pool version info\n\n<function>Memcached::getVersion<\/function> returns an array containing the version info for all available memcache servers.",null,["array|false","Array of server versions, one entry per server."]]
["function","Increment numeric item's value\n\n<function>Memcached::increment<\/function> increments a numeric item's value by the specified <parameter>offset<\/parameter>. If the item's value is not numeric, an error will result. <function>Memcached::increment<\/function> will set the item to the <parameter>initial_value<\/parameter> parameter if the key doesn't exist.",[["$key","string","The key of the item to increment."],["$offset","int","The amount by which to increment the item's value."],["$initial_value","int","The value to set the item to if it doesn't currently exist."],["$expiry","int","The expiry time to set on the item."]],["int|false","Returns new item's value on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::decrement","Memcached::decrementByKey","Memcached::incrementByKey"]]
["function","Increment numeric item's value, stored on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::incrementByKey<\/function> increments a numeric item's value by the specified <parameter>offset<\/parameter>. If the item's value is not numeric, an error will result. <function>Memcached::incrementByKey<\/function> will set the item to the <parameter>initial_value<\/parameter> parameter if the key doesn't exist.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key of the item to increment."],["$offset","int","The amount by which to increment the item's value."],["$initial_value","int","The value to set the item to if it doesn't currently exist."],["$expiry","int","The expiry time to set on the item."]],["int|false","Returns new item's value on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::decrement","Memcached::decrementByKey","Memcached::increment"]]
["function","Check if a persistent connection to memcache is being used\n\n<function>Memcached::isPersistent<\/function> checks if the connections to the memcache servers are persistent connections.",null,["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> if Memcache instance uses a persistent connection, <type>false<\/type> otherwise."],["Memcached::isPristine"]]
["function","Check if the instance was recently created\n\n<function>Memcached::isPristine<\/function> checks if the Memcache instance was recently created.",null,["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> if instance is recently created, <type>false<\/type> otherwise."],["Memcached::isPersistent"]]
["function","Prepend data to an existing item\n\n<function>Memcached::prepend<\/function> prepends the given <parameter>value<\/parameter> string to the value of an existing item. The reason that <parameter>value<\/parameter> is forced to be a string is that prepending mixed types is not well-defined.\n\nIf the <constant>Memcached::OPT_COMPRESSION<\/constant> is enabled, the operation will fail and a warning will be issued, because prepending compressed data to a value that is potentially already compressed is not possible.",[["$key","string","The key of the item to prepend the data to."],["$value","string","The string to prepend."]],["bool|null","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Returns <type>null<\/type> when compression is on."],["Memcached::prependByKey","Memcached::append"]]
["function","Prepend data to an existing item on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::prependByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::prepend, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key of the item to prepend the data to."],["$value","string","The string to prepend."]],["bool|null","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Returns <type>null<\/type> when compression is on."],["Memcached::prepend","Memcached::append"]]
["function","Close any open connections\n\n<function>Memcached::quit<\/function> closes any open connections to the memcache servers.",null,["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."]]
["function","Replace the item under an existing key\n\n<function>Memcached::replace<\/function> is similar to Memcached::set, but the operation fails if the <parameter>key<\/parameter> does not exist on the server.",[["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTSTORED<\/constant> if the key does not exist."],["Memcached::replaceByKey","Memcached::set","Memcached::add"]]
["function","Replace the item under an existing key on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::replaceByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::replace, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server. This is useful if you need to keep a bunch of related keys on a certain server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. The Memcached::getResultCode will return <constant>Memcached::RES_NOTSTORED<\/constant> if the key does not exist."],["Memcached::replace","Memcached::set","Memcached::add"]]
["function","Clears all servers from the server list\n\n<function>Memcached::resetserverlist<\/function> removes all memcache servers from the known server list, resetting it back to empty.",null,["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::addServer","Memcached::addServers"]]
["function","Store an item\n\n<function>Memcached::set<\/function> stores the <parameter>value<\/parameter> on a memcache server under the specified <parameter>key<\/parameter>. The <parameter>expiration<\/parameter> parameter can be used to control when the value is considered expired.\n\nThe value can be any valid PHP type except for resources, because those cannot be represented in a serialized form. If the <constant>Memcached::OPT_COMPRESSION<\/constant> option is turned on, the serialized value will also be compressed before storage.",[["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::setByKey","Memcached::add","Memcached::replace"]]
["function","Store an item on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::setByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::set, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server. This is useful if you need to keep a bunch of related keys on a certain server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$value","mixed","The value to store."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::set"]]
["function","Set AES encryption key for data in Memcached\n\nThis method sets the AES encryption\/decryption key for data written to and read from Memcached.",[["$key","string","The AES key."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::get","Memcached::add","Memcached::set"]]
["function","Store multiple items\n\n<function>Memcached::setMulti<\/function> is similar to Memcached::set, but instead of a single key\/value item, it works on multiple items specified in <parameter>items<\/parameter>. The <parameter>expiration<\/parameter> time applies to all the items at once.",[["$items","array","An array of key\/value pairs to store on the server."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::setMultiByKey","Memcached::set"]]
["function","Store multiple items on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::setMultiByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::setMulti, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the keys from <parameter>items<\/parameter> to a specific server. This is useful if you need to keep a bunch of related keys on a certain server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$items","array","An array of key\/value pairs to store on the server."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::setMulti","Memcached::set"]]
["function","Set a Memcached option\n\nThis method sets the value of a Memcached <parameter>option<\/parameter>. Some options correspond to the ones defined by libmemcached, and some are specific to the extension.",[["$option","int","One of the <constant>Memcached::OPT_*<\/constant> constant. See Memcached Constants for more information."],["$value","mixed","The value to be set.\n\nThe options listed below require values specified via constants. <constant>Memcached::OPT_HASH<\/constant> requires <constant>Memcached::HASH_*<\/constant> values. <constant>Memcached::OPT_DISTRIBUTION<\/constant> requires <constant>Memcached::DISTRIBUTION_*<\/constant> values. <constant>Memcached::OPT_SERIALIZER<\/constant> requires <constant>Memcached::SERIALIZER_*<\/constant> values. <constant>Memcached::OPT_COMPRESSION_TYPE<\/constant> requires <constant>Memcached::COMPRESSION_*<\/constant> values. "]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::getOption","Memcached::setOptions","Memcached Constants"]]
["function","Set Memcached options\n\n<function>Memcached::setOptions<\/function> is a variation of the Memcached::setOption that takes an array of options to be set.",[["$options","array","An associative array of options where the key is the option to set and the value is the new value for the option."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."],["Memcached::getOption","Memcached::setOption","Memcached Constants"]]
["function","Set the credentials to use for authentication\n\n<function>Memcached::setSaslAuthData<\/function> sets the username and password that should be used for SASL authentication with the memcache servers.\n\nThis method is only available when the memcached extension is built with SASL support. Please refer to Memcached setup for how to do this.",[["$username","string","The username to use for authentication."],["$password","string","The password to use for authentication."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure."]]
["function","Set a new expiration on an item\n\n<function>Memcached::touch<\/function> sets a new expiration value on the given key.",[["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::touchByKey"]]
["function","Set a new expiration on an item on a specific server\n\n<function>Memcached::touchByKey<\/function> is functionally equivalent to Memcached::touch, except that the free-form <parameter>server_key<\/parameter> can be used to map the <parameter>key<\/parameter> to a specific server.",[["$server_key","string","The key identifying the server to store the value on or retrieve it from. Instead of hashing on the actual key for the item, we hash on the server key when deciding which memcached server to talk to. This allows related items to be grouped together on a single server for efficiency with multi operations."],["$key","string","The key under which to store the value."],["$expiration","int","The expiration time, defaults to 0. See Expiration Times for more info."]],["bool","Returns <type>true<\/type> on success or <type>false<\/type> on failure. Use Memcached::getResultCode if necessary."],["Memcached::touch"]]
["class","MessageFormatter is a concrete class that enables users to produce concatenated, language-neutral messages. The methods supplied in this class are used to build all the messages that are seen by end users.\n\nThe MessageFormatter class assembles messages from various fragments (such as text fragments, numbers, and dates) supplied by the program. Because of the MessageFormatter class, the program does not need to know the order of the fragments. The class uses the formatting specifications for the fragments to assemble them into a message that is contained in a single string within a resource bundle. For example, MessageFormatter enables you to print the phrase \"Finished printing x out of y files...\" in a manner that still allows for flexibility in translation.\n\nPreviously, an end user message was created as a sentence and handled as a string. This procedure created problems for localizers because the sentence structure, word order, number format and so on are very different from language to language. The language-neutral way to create messages keeps each part of the message separate and provides keys to the data. Using these keys, the MessageFormatter class can concatenate the parts of the message, localize them, and display a well-formed string to the end user.\n\nMessageFormatter takes a set of objects, formats them, and then inserts the formatted strings into the pattern at the appropriate places. Choice formats can be used in conjunction with MessageFormatter to handle plurals, match numbers, and select from an array of items. Typically, the message format will come from resources and the arguments will be dynamically set at runtime."]
["function","Constructs a new Message Formatter\n\nObject-oriented style (method)\n\nObject-oriented style (constructor):\n\nProcedural style\n\nConstructs a new Message Formatter",[["$locale","string","The locale to use when formatting arguments"],["$pattern","string","The pattern string to stick arguments into. The pattern uses an 'apostrophe-friendly' syntax; see Quoting\/Escaping for details."]],["MessageFormatter|null","The formatter <type>object<\/type>, or <type>null<\/type> on failure."],["<function>msgfmt_format<\/function>","<function>msgfmt_parse<\/function>","<function>msgfmt_get_error_code<\/function>","<function>msgfmt_get_error_message<\/function>"]]
